Keep clang's newer default-error C diagnostics as warnings for vendored C

Everything xenia itself writes is C++; the COMPILE_LANGUAGE:C block only
reaches the third-party C it vendors, chiefly its FFmpeg submodule, which it
already compiles with -w because it does not own that code. That is also why
the block already carries -Wno-implicit-function-declaration: clang 15 turned
that diagnostic into an error by default and the vendored C had not caught up.

Clang has since done the same to two more: -Wint-conversion (clang 16) and
-Wincompatible-pointer-types (clang 21). Building against LLVM 22 therefore
fails on pre-C99-era constructs in code xenia deliberately does not police, and
-w cannot help because it silences warnings without demoting errors.

Demote both to warnings, which -w then silences, matching the treatment the
implicit-function-declaration case already gets. -Wno-error= rather than -Wno=
so the diagnostics survive for anyone who drops -w.

Signed-off-by: Carlos Martins <cjom@reglinux.org>

diff --git a/CMakeLists.txt b/CMakeLists.txt
--- a/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -181,6 +181,8 @@
   # C-specific warnings
   add_compile_options(
     $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:C>:-Wno-implicit-function-declaration>
+    $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:C>:-Wno-error=incompatible-pointer-types>
+    $<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:C>:-Wno-error=int-conversion>
   )
 
   # GTK3 via pkg-config
